# The bare functions (ADR 0093): Ruby's `Kernel`, as top-level defs. The
# core loads ahead of every program in one environment, so a def here is
# callable bare, and there is no `Kernel` module to qualify it with.
#
# `puts`, `p`, `print`, and `format` take any number of arguments, which a
# def cannot spell while splat parameters are deferred (ADR 0014), so they
# stay in the floor.

# `putc` writes one character — an Integer's low byte, or a String's first
# code point — and answers its argument, as Ruby's does (#185).
def putc(character)
  case character
  in Integer
    System.write_standard_output(character.bit_and(255).chr(Encoding::UTF_8))
  in String
    first = character.codepoints.first
    System.write_standard_output(first.chr(Encoding::UTF_8)) unless first.nil?
  else
    panic("putc takes an integer or a string, got #{inspect(character)}")
  end
  character
end
